%% Sequence diagram of one federated round (chain_* and ours_* methods, and the live demo).
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant H as Hospital (each of K)
  participant FC as Aggregator (FoldCommitter)
  participant KR as Key registry (KeyRegistry)
  participant SC as Screen (Forensics)
  participant AN as Anchor (ChainAnchor)
  participant BC as Ledger (Blockchain)
  H->>AN: Is the previous round still on the ledger? (MeteredAnchor.verify_previous())
  AN->>BC: Re-check every block (is_chain_valid())
  BC-->>AN: Valid, and the block still holds the previous root
  AN-->>H: Go ahead
  H->>H: Train on own patients (dp_sgd_epochs())
  H->>H: Serialize, content ID, sign (serialize_lr(), content_id(), sign_submission())
  H->>FC: Signed submission and update bytes (FoldCommitter.submit())
  FC->>KR: Right key, this round, fresh counter? (KeyRegistry.verify())
  KR-->>FC: Accept, or reject with a reason (replay, wrong key, ...)
  FC->>FC: Merkle root over the accepted digests (round_merkle_root(), inclusion_proof())
  FC->>AN: Anchor the round root (ChainAnchor.anchor_round())
  AN->>BC: Add the transactions and mine a block (Blockchain.mine_pending_transactions())
  BC-->>AN: Block hash, used as the transaction id
  FC->>FC: Rebuild each update from its committed bytes (deserialize())
  FC->>SC: Score the committed updates (Forensics.aggregate())
  SC-->>FC: Accepted / rejected, and which score fired
  FC-->>H: New shared model, the average of the accepted updates
  Note over FC,BC: rounds.jsonl and commitments.jsonl record every step, and the dashboard server re-derives each root (commit_round_route())
